nodecode-fusion is a rewrite of BoredSexyJordan's BoredSexyJordan/hermes-fusion:

  https://github.com/BoredSexyJordan/hermes-fusion
  commit edab6a6d17838d4d9a1525a2a7d3500c62694e17
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Jordan Rosenberg (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the detection modules (harnesses.py, classify.py, roster.py,
paths.py) and the packet validator (scripts/validate_fusion_packet.py) as one
Common Lisp cell. It reads the same four harness configs, tiers every
(provider, model) with the original's frontier/cheap name heuristics, builds
the same five rosters from what it finds, and validates v3/v4 packets rule for
rule — the frontier/cheap hint lists and the roster shapes are carried over
literally. Detection reads the files itself rather than through a YAML or TOML
library, so it understands the shapes Hermes and Codex write rather than every
document those formats allow.

Left out: the `run` executor with its adapters (clean session, kanban handoff,
delegate task), which spawn the Hermes binary once per team member and so need
Hermes itself; the model index sync, which reads Hermes' provider table into a
JSON index; the feedback report and the run registry; the MCP server; and the
subagent prompts and the `/fusion <task>` slash command that made an agent
orchestrate a run. What is left is the half that answers offline: what this
machine can call, how the original would tier and compose it, and whether a
plan packet is well formed.
